              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 27,
                "start": 25
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Government, it proposes a novel interpretation of the saving clause, arguing that the phrase \u201cinadequate or ineffective\u201d refers only to the ability to test claims that are cognizable under the general habeas statutes, not to statutory\u2011injustice claims like Jones\u2019s."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Government",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "7fc24aa16225dbf8d0062f3010102176f90566c52d691205dba91358ab22b19d",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-7fc24aa16225-0002-0006"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 27,
                "start": 27
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "The Government also argues that allowing a statutory\u2011injustice claim would create \u201chighly counterintuitive\u201d results and that Congress did not intend the saving clause to function as a \u201cRube\u2011Goldberg contrivance.\u201d"
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Respondent (Government)",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
